# Walk the user through the rules Minsky installed

`minsky init` installs Minsky's **base** rules plus its **optional** ones, and returns the
optional list. Base rules cannot be declined — Minsky does not work without them. The optional
ones are the user's call, and **they stay installed until someone asks.** Asking is your job.

## When this fires

Right after `minsky init` runs in this project, and any time the user asks what rules they have
or wants to change them. If `init` reported no optional rules, there is nothing to ask about —
say so in one line and move on.

## What to do

1. **Read the optional list from `init`'s result.** It carries each rule's id and a one-line
   description. Under the MCP tool the list is the `declinable` field; on the command line it is
   printed in the output. Do not go looking for the files — the list is handed to you.
2. **Present it to the user in one pass, not one question per rule.** Give each rule its id and
   its description in a single message, and ask which — if any — they want to turn off. Use
   whatever question affordance your harness has. A user who says "keep them all" is finished.
3. **Record each choice as they make it:** `minsky rules disable --id <id>` to decline,
   `minsky rules enable --id <id>` to re-enable one they declined earlier.
4. **Recompile once, at the end:** `minsky compile`. This is what actually removes a declined
   rule from the files your harness reads. Skipping it leaves the config saying one thing and
   the harness loading another.

## What not to do

- **Do not decline anything on the user's behalf**, and do not recommend a default set. Which
  rules a project wants is a preference, not a technical judgment, and the whole point of the
  list is that they chose it.
- **Do not hand-edit `.claude/rules/`, `.cursor/rules/`, `AGENTS.md`, or `CLAUDE.md`.** Those are
  compiled outputs; the next `minsky compile` overwrites them. Selection lives in
  `.minsky/config.yaml`, written by `rules enable|disable`.
- **Do not treat a refusal as an error.** Declining a base rule is refused on purpose, with a
  reason — relay the reason rather than retrying.

## Reversal

Every choice here is reversible at any time: `minsky rules enable --id <id>` then `minsky compile`
restores a rule the user declined, and `minsky rules config` shows what is currently selected.
Say so when you ask — a user deciding under the impression that a choice is permanent will
decline nothing.
